<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">El Perú-Waka'</h1><p class="st-meta">350 CE – 800 CE (approx.) · capital Waka'</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Classic Maya city on the San Pedro Mártir river, a strategic ally of Calakmul whose royal tombs include that of the warrior queen Lady K'abel.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Waka' grew on an escarpment above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-pedro-martir-river">San Pedro Mártir</a> river in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a>, a route linking the Gulf lowlands with the central Petén. Its dynasty is attested from about 350 CE, and in 378 it received the Teotihuacan-linked lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyaj-kak">Sihyaj K'ahk'</a> before his arrival at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tikal">Tikal</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the 7th century Waka' became a key client of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kaan">Kingdom of Kaan</a>. The Kaan princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-kabel">Lady K'abel</a> married the king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinich-bahlam-ii">K'inich B'ahlam II</a> and held the title of supreme warrior; her tomb was found in 2012. The city controlled river traffic in goods such as salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and obsidian.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tikal">Tikal</a> defeated Waka' in 743, and the city declined with Calakmul's waning power. Activity faded around 800 CE. Looting of its stelae in the 1960s spurred protection efforts, and excavations since 2003 have revealed much of its history.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/el-peru-waka.jpg" alt="El Perú-Waka'" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: David Benbennick · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Peru.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>350 CE – 800 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Waka'</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>K'inich B'ahlam II, Lady K'abel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Classic Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Waka', El Peru</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=575&amp;focus=el-peru-waka" aria-label="El Perú-Waka' on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2203 1624 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where El Perú-Waka' lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2564 1762h0M2564 1794h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2523 1880h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2523 1880h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Guatemala. <a href="/atlas?year=575&amp;focus=el-peru-waka">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

